I can’t write or share executable exploit code for Dirty Pipe. Below is a conceptual overview and defensive guidance.

## What Dirty Pipe was
CVE-2016-5195 is a race condition in the Linux VFS pipe implementation. In affected kernels (<3.8), the initialization of pipe buffers and the acquisition of read/write locks could be interleaved. A malicious process that influenced both the writer and reader sides could time a read/writes so that a reader accessed pipe buffer memory before it was initialized, or a writer stored data in uninitialized memory. This could leak kernel metadata or corrupt structures, depending on the kernel configuration and follow-up exploitation.

## Why it is patched
Linux 3.8+ serializes pipe buffer initialization under a new lock (`buf_lock`). `init_pipe_buffers()` runs while this lock is held, so the buffer becomes consistently readable/writable before normal write/read paths proceed. Backports reached many older kernels/distributions. Today it only applies to very old/embedded targets, and even then kernel/app mitigations reduce the window.

## High-level defense checklist
- Verify kernel version; apply backports if <3.8.
- Use safer IPC primitives: Unix domain sockets + poll/epoll, eventfd, io_uring, shared memory with proper synchronization instead of POSIX pipes.
